<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sisodia dynasty</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Sisodias</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sisodia">Sisodias</a> are a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajputs">Rajput</a> clan, a branch of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guhila-dynasty">Guhila dynasty</a> named after the village of Sisoda, that ruled Mewar from the recovery of Chittor by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hammir-singh">Hammir Singh</a> around 1326 until 1949. Its rulers, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rana-kumbha">Rana Kumbha</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rana-sanga">Rana Sanga</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maharana-pratap">Maharana Pratap</a>, reigned from Chittor and later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/udaipur">Udaipur</a> and became famous for their long resistance to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi-sultanate">Delhi Sultanate</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughals</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sisodia-dynasty.jpg" alt="Sisodia dynasty" width="320" height="216" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown authorUnknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Coat_of_arms_of_Udaipur_State.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
